  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Sunday...\n\nNorth/northeast winds will advect in a band of wildfire smoke\ntoday. Looking at observations of air quality to our north,\ndangerous air quality is not expected, though members of sensitive\ngroups may experience health effects with 24 hours of exposure.\nModels currently predict that the cold front will push the\nwildfire smoke to our south with cleaner air expected by Tuesday\nmorning. Expect cooler temperatures today in the high 80s and low\n90s F with winds gusting to near 30 mph. \n\nTuesday will see continued cooler conditions with high temperatures\nin the 80s F. Upper level ridging begins to build back Tuesday \nallowing monsoonal moisture to return bringing 30-40% chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms along the Wyoming border. Wednesday and \nThursday are expected to bring more widespread 30-60% chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms with temperatures in the high 80s to low\n90s F. High PWAT values of 150-225% of normal will create an \nelevated risk for extremely heavy rainfall and associated flash \nflooding risk these days.\n\nUpper level ridging is expected to continue Friday through the\nweekend. There are some model differences in the details however.\nEnsemble systems are showing strong ridging while deterministic \nmodels are indicating a potential ridge crasher. The ridge \ncrasher scenario has the potential to bring elevated fire \nconditions. What is not in question is that Friday through Sunday\nwill be hot with triple digit heat likely (50-90%) for the lower\nelevations. Torgerson",
